Hi Jason,

regarding the intercept, I cannot help you more. I am sorry. I think this depends on the model and what you really want to know. From the paper it did not become clear to me how to do it. In my former reply I did not distinguish between one and two replicates setting but used two different models for the same data. One includes a random intercept for each gene and a random effect for each gene, which is what I understood from the paper, and the second has a common intercept for all genes and then a random effect to model gene-specific deviations from this overall level.

Best,
Andrea

On Saturday, December 13, 2014 3:42:09 PM UTC+1, jason....@gmail.com wrote:
> Dear Andrea,
>
> Thanks for your reply. It helped a lot.
>
> So for one-replicate dataset, the model would be
>
> formula <- paternal ~ 1 + f(idx, model="iid")
> result <- inla(formula, family="binomial", data=data, Ntrials=Ntrials)
>
> I can use result$summary.random$idx to extract the estimation for each gene. And the result looks good for my data. However, I still have one question - whether to request the regression through the origin or not. Because the result is slightly different from the following model:
>
> formula <- paternal ~ -1 + f(idx, model="iid")
> result <- inla(formula, family="binomial", data=data, Ntrials=Ntrials)
>
> In your reply, you use +1 for one-replicate model, but -1 for two-replicate model. Could you explain a little bit about this?
>
> Best regards,
> Jason
>
>
>
> On Friday, December 12, 2014 11:04:11 AM UTC+1, Andrea Riebler wrote:
> > Hi Jason,
> >
> > I think epsilon_j should be estimated and thus be included as a random effect. However, I am actually not sure what the exact model formulation of Connelly et al, 2014, Plos Genetics is and tried to derive it from their paper on page 9. It looks like that they have a separate intercept for each j, which seems a bit strange as the included an additional iid random effect epsilon_j as well.  The model would be
> >
> > Ntrials <- apply(data, 1, sum)
> > data$idx <- 1:nrow(data)
> > data$idx2 <- 1:nrow(data)
> > formula <- paternal ~ factor(idx2) + f(idx, model="iid") -1
> > result <- inla(formula, family="binomial", data=data, Ntrials=Ntrials)
> >
> > and you could inspect the intercepts using summary(result) or result$summary.fixed. Alternatively, having only one intercept and the random effect epsilon_j the model should be
> >
> > formula <- paternal ~ 1 + f(idx, model="iid")
> > result <- inla(formula, family="binomial", data=data, Ntrials=Ntrials)
> >
> > However, then you would need to look at the results for the linear predictor in order to get a result for "each row", i.e.
> >
> > result$summary.linear.predictor
> >
> > I am not sure, if you are aware of the work of Mark van de Wiel
> >
